<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Do NOT empty this file. The PlistAndHelpTests tests pin the key below.

    Why: the release binary is signed with the Hardened Runtime. Apple documents
    com.apple.security.automation.apple-events as the entitlement that lets an app
    prompt the user for permission to send Apple events to other apps (it is not
    needed when an app only sends to itself or to processes with the same team ID).
    Things 3 belongs to another team, so this server needs it.

    Evidence level, kept honest on purpose: for Calendar, che-ical-mcp issue 154
    observed on macOS 26.5.1 that a Hardened Runtime binary without the matching
    entitlement gets NO permission prompt at all (a silent, permanent denial).
    That the same happens for Apple Events is inferred from Apple's documentation,
    not yet observed on this project. If you observe it, write it down in the
    issue tracker.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
